{"about":"9843f16c55a02ce422d04eb9057d52cb554dfe0052e19a3d19653ba96e18dc92","body":"The definition of a 'severe test' inherently relies on probabilistic reasoning: a test is severe only if it is unlikely that a false theory would pass it. By rejecting probability entirely while retaining the concept of severity, the conjecture creates a logical vacuum where the metric for 'better-corroborated' loses its quantitative force. Without the notion that passing a severe test makes a theory more probable (or at least less likely to be false by chance), the ranking of theories becomes unintelligible, as there is no formal mechanism to distinguish between a theory that survived a trivial test and one that survived a severe one, other than an informal intuition that smuggles probability back in.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The coherence of 'severe tests' without probability"}